{"duration_api_ms":99139,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"e699a048-21a2-4c40-a968-1b26b2abbd02","total_cost_usd":0.46430540000000003,"usage":{"input_tokens":12,"cache_creation_input_tokens":23631,"cache_read_input_tokens":136847,"output_tokens":12392,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":856},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":23631,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":887,"cache_read_input_tokens":33042,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1423,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1423},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5-5":{"inputTokens":12,"outputTokens":12392,"cacheReadInputTokens":136847,"cacheCreationInputTokens":23631,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.46430540000000003,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":856,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"safety_stops":0,"is_error":false,"num_turns":6,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"I wrote the test suite in `tests/test_agent.py`, and it found a real bug in the library: `unescape()` decodes `~01` wrongly. Six tests check that case and fail. I didn't change the library, so I marked them as expected failures instead. The run now shows **186 passed, 6 xfailed**.\n\n**The bug:** `unescape()` (`jsonpointer.py`, last line) replaces `~0` before `~1`. RFC 6901 §4 says to decode `~1` first so that `~01` becomes `~1`. The library turns it into `/` instead. As a result:\n- A key literally named `~1` can't be reached: `resolve_pointer({\"~1\": \"x\", \"/\": \"y\"}, \"/~01\")` returns `\"y\"`.\n- `JsonPointer(\"/~01\").path` comes back as `\"/~1\"`, so the pointer doesn't survive a round trip.\n- `JsonPointer.from_parts([\"~1\"])` builds a pointer whose parts are `[\"/\"]`.\n\nThe fix is a one-line swap: `s.replace('~1', '/').replace('~0', '~')`.\n\n**How I handled the failing tests:** you asked for passing tests and no library changes, so I marked the six tests that hit this bug `xfail(strict=True)`, all with one shared marker, `UNESCAPE_ORDER_BUG`. They still assert the correct RFC behaviour. Once the library is fixed they'll pass and break the run, which is your cue to delete the marker. If you'd rather the suite fail outright until the bug is fixed, remove the marker now.\n\n**What the suite covers:**\n- **Parsing:** `escape`/`unescape`, parsing a pointer into its parts, and rejecting bad escapes or a missing leading `/`.\n- **Resolving:** every example from RFC 6901 §5, nested paths, and default values (including falsy ones like `0` and `\"\"`).\n- **List indexes:** bad indexes (`01`, `-1`, `+1`, spaces, non-ASCII digits) and out-of-range ones, plus `-` as end-of-list.\n- **Other document types:** strings and plain values that can't be stepped into, and objects that only support `[]` lookup.\n- **Error messages:** `VERBOSE_EXCEPTIONS` on and off.\n- **Setting values:** in place and on a copy, replacing the root, appending with `-`, and assigning into tuples or past the end of a list.\n- **Pointer operations:** `contains`/`in`, `join`/`/`, `path`/`str`/`repr`, equality and hashing, `from_parts`, keeping subclass types, and `EndOfList`.","ttft_ms":2025,"type":"result","duration_ms":100904,"uuid":"1dfb9c9c-6b8d-4d63-aebc-8027b91340ca","ttft_stream_ms":1545,"time_to_request_ms":194,"first_content_frame_ms":1545,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
